9a6980216e821097024487dc65669dd1810309bd108144eba71" "basis" 2094 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/assets/770cdf03e988f811ccc3ff805889cef55304b01b6e8009e25ba128fbdbb89066.png)\n\nFind the volume of the solid region above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/state.json" "file_sha256" "217ab6b089dab9a6980216e821097024487dc65669dd1810309bd108144eba71" "basis" 2094 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/assets/8e5f3095cac4e068e54d9b3ac2bb55dc4789e7ca68d4524dce4d25a55abd5be6.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type III region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-2x-4z},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane.\n\nTo find the top boundary curve of $D$ in the form $z=z(x),$ we substitute $y=0$  into the equation $y=4-2x-4z:$\n\n$$\n0=4-2x-4z\\,⟹\\,z=1-\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-\\frac{x}{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/assets/76fcca4251a904a08ac2f6ba8181d92c2c2fa7e07e596ef6276f1d21b574817a.png)\n\nHence, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-\\frac{x}{2},\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-2x-4z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-2x-4z}dy\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/2}[y]_{y=0}^{y=4-2x-4z}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/2}(4-2x-4z)-0\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/2}4-2x-4z\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[(4-2x)z-2{z}^{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=1-x/2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}((4-2x)(1-\\frac{x}{2})-2{(1-\\frac{x}{2})}^{2})-0\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(1-\\frac{x}{2})((4-2x)-2(1-\\frac{x}{2}))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}(2-x)(2-x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2-2x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={[2x-{x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(2(2)-{2}^{2}+\\frac{{2}^{3}}{6})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/state.json" "file_sha256" "217ab6b089dab9a6980216e821097024487dc65669dd1810309bd108144eba71" "basis" 2094 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44954716125f0fe9cdf4719fc91e1d6cbf920b220a1bde53d11db47b973b90dc" "basis" 2094 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161742" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "c