ure" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/state.json" "file_sha256" "217ab6b089dab9a6980216e821097024487dc65669dd1810309bd108144eba71" "basis" 2094 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3-\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}-4{z}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/state.json" "file_sha256" "217ab6b089dab9a6980216e821097024487dc65669dd1810309bd108144eba71" "basis" 2094 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8189" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the volume of the solid $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR = \\Big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq \\sqrt{2y}, \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq yz \\Big\\}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/example-8189.json" "file_sha256" "442f40bebc2e80f57c8d12042a5391fcdf86c57f054488cf97bd01034c97ce8d" "basis" 2094 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8189" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that we have a three-dimensional type II region.\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2y}}{\\int }_{0}^{yz}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2y}}[x]_{x=0}^{x=yz}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2y}}yz\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y{[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=\\sqrt{2y}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{y}{2}{(\\sqrt{2y})}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=1}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(8-1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/example-8189.json" "file_sha256" "442f40bebc2e80f57c8d12042a5391fcdf86c57f054488cf97bd01034c97ce8d" "basis" 2094 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/assets/043adf0ef683c24fbf4680145411d86fa94b52016bee44ac8f08ca7c5ca9f1b7.png)\n\n \n\nFind the volume of the solid region above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/example-8190.json" "file_sha256" "6a88fdad7584276fc1940ea3b30f436357b187624ed13b9ac2fa720177a5cb90" "basis" 2094 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/assets/75a76a2dbc34c889335e66623e12b686eb9a255ee0d67f9be0e3792f2650360c.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 4-x-y \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/assets/e19ffe6a0555851c2b05526272beb21dbcdc082a23a2e11809c661666efdf133.png)\n\nHence, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 4-x-y \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-x-y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-x-y)-0\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4-x-y\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[(4-x)y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((4-x)(2)-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})-0\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6-2x\\,dx \\\\  & =[6x-{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(6(1)-{1}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/example-8190.json" "file_sha256" "6a88fdad7584276fc1940ea3b30f436357b187624ed13b9ac2fa720177a5cb90" "basis" 2094 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the volume of the solid region enclosed by the surface ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064036/assets/9597cda64435e7e578c996c4c5d3e3056705e5c4fb79ccbb9bbf48cd7befe296.png) the plane ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/140640